<h1>Walk-Forward Backtesting</h1>
<div class="why">
  <strong>Why it matters for this project:</strong>
  The flagship model in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> is trained once on all history and tested on the same data — a shortcut fine for prototyping, but it overfits.
  <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> adds the production pattern: retrain on a rolling window, predict only into the strictly unseen future.
  <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/risk.py:walkforward_splits()</code> is the pure function behind that logic.
  Understanding <em>why</em> k-fold CV fails on time series — and what walk-forward fixes — is the single most important statistical discipline in quant research.
</div>

