<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SECTION 1 — The core problem: look-ahead bias
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>1. The Core Problem: Look-Ahead Bias</h2>
<label class="section-check" data-sec="0"><input type="checkbox">Mark section complete</label>

<p>A backtest is a simulation run on historical data to estimate how a strategy <em>would have</em> performed. The cardinal sin is letting information from the future leak into a past decision. This is <strong>look-ahead bias</strong>, and it makes backtests look far better than live trading ever will.</p>

<p>Look-ahead bias appears in two layers in an ML backtest:</p>

<h3>Layer 1 — Feature/target leakage</h3>
<p>The most obvious form. In <code>paper_trading_sim.py:build_features</code>:</p>

<pre><span class="cm"># paper_trading_sim.py:62–68</span>
out[<span class="str">"ret_1d"</span>]    = out[<span class="str">"close"</span>].<span class="fn">pct_change</span>()
out[<span class="str">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>] = out[<span class="str">"ret_1d"</span>].<span class="fn">shift</span>(<span class="num">-1</span>)   <span class="cm"># tomorrow's return as today's label</span></pre>

<p>The <code>shift(-1)</code> is the correct discipline. If you accidentally used <code>shift(0)</code> — attaching <em>today's</em> return as today's label — the model would predict the past and look spectacular in backtests.</p>

<h3>Layer 2 — Model training leakage</h3>
<p>Subtler and more common. If you train a model on all available data, <em>then</em> backtest it on that same data, the model has seen the future during training — it memorized the test period. Reported Sharpe is fictional.</p>

<div class="box box-warn">
  <h4>⚠ The standard bad pattern</h4>
  <strong>Train on 2016–2024 → Backtest on 2016–2024</strong><br>
  The model's training data includes the very days you're "testing." Any error signal in the training loss is from those same days. In-sample metrics are inflation, not performance.
</div>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SECTION 2 — Why k-fold CV fails
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>2. Why Standard k-Fold Cross-Validation Fails</h2>
<label class="section-check" data-sec="1"><input type="checkbox">Mark section complete</label>

<p>In classic ML you use <strong>k-fold cross-validation</strong>: shuffle all data, split into k equal folds, hold each fold out in turn, train on the other k−1. This works when rows are independent (images, survey responses). It <em>breaks</em> on financial time series.</p>

<h3>The problem: test folds before training data</h3>

<div class="timeline-wrap">
  <div style="font-size:.75rem;font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:.4rem">
    5-fold CV on time-series data (rows: day 1 → day 250):
  </div>
  <!-- k-fold rows showing the problem -->
  <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:.3rem;min-width:600px">
