      <div class="tl-seg tl-train" style="width:320px">TRAIN</div>
      <span style="color:var(--red);margin-left:.5rem;font-size:.7rem">← test days 1-50, train on days 51-250 ✓ ok</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1px;align-items:center;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--mono)">
      <span style="width:60px;color:var(--muted)">Fold 2</span>
      <div class="tl-seg tl-train" style="width:80px">TRAIN</div>
      <div class="tl-seg kfold-danger" style="width:80px">TEST</div>
      <div class="tl-seg tl-train" style="width:240px">TRAIN</div>
      <span style="color:var(--red);margin-left:.5rem;font-size:.7rem">← train on days 151-250 (future!) ✗ LEAK</span>
    </div>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1px;align-items:center;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--mono)">
      <span style="width:60px;color:var(--muted)">Fold 3</span>
      <div class="tl-seg tl-train" style="width:160px">TRAIN</div>
      <div class="tl-seg kfold-danger" style="width:80px">TEST</div>
      <div class="tl-seg tl-train" style="width:160px">TRAIN</div>
      <span style="color:var(--red);margin-left:.5rem;font-size:.7rem">← train on days 201-250 (future!) ✗ LEAK</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>For folds 2–5, the training set includes data <em>after</em> the test period. The model learns from the future. Cross-validation scores are inflated and rank models incorrectly.</p>

<p>There's also an <strong>autocorrelation</strong> problem: adjacent days share features (rolling vol, MA, RSI all overlap). Even if you use strict time ordering, adjacent test/train splits contaminate each other through correlated features — which is why the <em>embargo</em> gap exists.</p>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SECTION 3 — Walk-forward: the correct pattern
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>3. Walk-Forward: Always Predict Only the Strict Future</h2>
<label class="section-check" data-sec="2"><input type="checkbox">Mark section complete</label>

<p>Walk-forward backtesting enforces one rule: <strong>the training window always ends before the test window begins</strong>. The window then rolls forward in steps equal to the test period size.</p>

