<h3>Two variants: rolling vs. expanding window</h3>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Property</th><th>Rolling (fixed-length) window</th><th>Expanding window</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Training data</td>
    <td>Always the last <em>N</em> days</td>
    <td>All history up to now</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Model assumed to be</td>
    <td>Non-stationary — old data hurts</td>
    <td>Stable over time — more data always helps</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Used in <code>walkforward_sim.py</code></td>
    <td class="yes">✓ default (<code>--train_window_days 756</code>)</td>
    <td>Not directly, but remove the <code>sub.iloc[-train_window_days:]</code> cut</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>Risk</td>
    <td>Forgets structural regime shifts if window is too long; overfits regime if too short</td>
    <td>Early-period model dominates if the relationship changed</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h3>The embargo gap</h3>

<p>Why leave a gap between train and test? The target is <code>fwd_ret_1d = ret.shift(-1)</code> — tomorrow's return at today's row. So the last training row "knows about" day <em>train+1</em>'s price. Rolling features (MA, vol, RSI) also use overlapping windows. Without an embargo, the first test row is directly contaminated by the last train label. A gap of ≥ 1 day (or ≥ feature window days for safety) breaks that contamination.</p>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SECTION 4 — In this project
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<h2>4. In This Project</h2>
<label class="section-check" data-sec="3"><input type="checkbox">Mark section complete</label>

<h3><code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/risk.py:75–95</code> — <code>walkforward_splits()</code></h3>

<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">walkforward_splits</span>(<span class="param">n</span>: int, <span class="param">train</span>: int, <span class="param">test</span>: int,
                       <span class="param">embargo</span>: int = <span class="num">0</span>) -> list[tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]]:
    <span class="str">"""Chronological walk-forward splits over `n` samples.

    Each split is (train_indices, test_indices): `train` consecutive samples,
    an `embargo` gap (left out entirely), then `test` consecutive samples.
    The window rolls forward by `test` so test windows tile without overlap.
    """</span>
    splits: list[tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]] = []
    start = <span class="num">0</span>
    <span class="kw">while</span> start + train + embargo + test <= n:
        tr = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(start, start + train)                     <span class="cm"># ① indices of training rows</span>
        te = np.<span class="fn">arange</span>(start + train + embargo,               <span class="cm"># ② skip the embargo gap</span>
                        start + train + embargo + test)
        splits.<span class="fn">append</span>((tr, te))
        start += test                                          <span class="cm"># ③ roll forward by one test window</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> splits</pre>

<p>Walk through it:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>① Train indices:</strong> rows <code>[start, start+train)</code> — always strictly in the past.</li>
  <li><strong>② Skip embargo:</strong> rows <code>[start+train, start+train+embargo)</code> are thrown away — no train, no test.</li>
  <li><strong>Test indices:</strong> rows <code>[start+train+embargo, start+train+embargo+test)</code> — true out-of-sample.</li>
  <li><strong>③ Roll:</strong> <code>start += test</code> tiles the test windows end-to-end so the whole dataset gets evaluated exactly once.</li>
</ol>

<div class="box box-green">
  <h4>Key property</h4>
  Every test row is evaluated using a model that was fitted on <em>strictly earlier</em> data, separated by the embargo. This is the same discipline as live trading: you will never know the future.
</div>

<h3><code>walkforward_sim.py:207–228</code> — Rolling retrain in production</h3>

<pre><span class="cm"># walkforward_sim.py — rolling retrain logic (simplified)</span>
<span class="kw">if</span> rolling_retrain <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="fn">_should_retrain</span>(last_train_dt, dt):
    tr_frames = []
    <span class="kw">for</span> t, f <span class="kw">in</span> feats.<span class="fn">items</span>():
        sub = f[f.index < dt]                        <span class="cm"># ← CRITICAL: strict past only</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> train_window_days > <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(sub) > train_window_days:
            sub = sub.<span class="fn">iloc</span>[-train_window_days:]      <span class="cm"># rolling window (discard old data)</span>
        tr_frames.<span class="fn">append</span>(sub)
    train_df = pd.<span class="fn">concat</span>(tr_frames, axis=<span class="num">0</span>)
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(train_df) >= min_train_days:
        model.<span class="fn">fit</span>(X_train, y_train)
        last_train_dt = dt</pre>

<p>The key line is <code>f[f.index &lt; dt]</code> — strictly less than, not ≤. On date <code>dt</code> the model sees every day <em>before</em> <code>dt</code> but never <code>dt</code> itself. This is the code-level enforcement of the walk-forward discipline.</p>

<h3>Contrast: <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> — the single-split baseline</h3>

<pre><span class="cm"># paper_trading_sim.py:backtest() — simple train/test split</span>
split_idx = int(<span class="fn">len</span>(feat) * args.train_frac)   <span class="cm"># e.g. 70% train</span>
train_feat, test_feat = <span class="fn">split_features</span>(feat, split_idx)
model = <span class="fn">train_model</span>(train_feat)
<span class="cm"># test on the remaining 30% — only evaluated once</span></pre>

<p>This is the single hold-out split: train once, test once. Fine for a first check; misleading because the choice of <code>train_frac</code> has a large effect on the result and you only see one test period. Walk-forward gives you many non-overlapping test periods, making the aggregate statistics far more trustworthy.</p>

