<h3>Walk-forward ≠ Paper trading</h3>
<p><em>Walk-forward backtesting</em> simulates the model retraining and prediction process on historical data to estimate live performance. <em>Paper trading</em> runs the strategy in real-time on live market data without placing orders. Walk-forward is retrospective; paper trading is prospective. Both are needed: walk-forward to design, paper trading to verify.</p>

<h3>In-sample R² ≠ Out-of-sample R²</h3>
<p>GBM can achieve in-sample R² > 0.5 on financial data (it memorizes). Walk-forward out-of-sample R² on daily equity returns is typically 0.01–0.05 — a few percent of variance explained. That small number is real alpha; the in-sample number is noise from overfitting. <em>Never report in-sample metrics as the backtest result.</em></p>

