<h2>7. Scenarios</h2>
<label class="section-check" data-sec="6"><input type="checkbox">Mark section complete</label>

<div class="scenario">
  <p><strong>Scenario A.</strong> A colleague adds a MinMaxScaler step before training: they fit the scaler on all 2016–2024 data, then use it to transform features both in the training and test folds of their walk-forward. Is this correct?</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Show answer →</summary>
    <div class="ans">
      <strong>No — this is scaler leakage.</strong> Fitting the scaler on all data means the scaler's <code>min_</code> and <code>max_</code> are computed using future test rows. The scaler now "knows" the range of prices in periods the model hasn't trained on yet. The correct pattern: fit the scaler only on the current training window, then <code>transform</code> (not <code>fit_transform</code>) on the test window. In <code>walkforward_sim.py</code> there is currently no explicit scaler — this is the form of leakage to watch for if you add feature normalization.
    </div>
  </details>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <p><strong>Scenario B.</strong> You call <code>walkforward_splits(n=500, train=250, test=50, embargo=0)</code>. How many splits? What would happen if you accidentally used the test rows from split 1 as part of the training data in split 2?</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Show answer →</summary>
    <div class="ans">
      <strong>Splits:</strong> start=0 → needs 0+250+0+50=300 ≤ 500 ✓; start=50 → 300 ≤ 500 ✓; ... continues every 50 rows. Splits at starts 0, 50, 100, 150, 200 → <strong>5 splits</strong> (start=250 needs 550 > 500 ✗).
      <br><br>
      <strong>If you used test rows from split 1 (250–299) in training for split 2:</strong> split 2's model would have been trained on data it later "predicts." The test Sharpe and R² for split 2 would be inflated. Across 5 splits, this contamination compounds. The walkforward_splits function avoids this by design: <code>start += test</code> means the next split's training starts at row 50, not row 0, so split 2's test window (350–399) has never appeared in training at start=50 (training rows 50–299, no embargo).
    </div>
  </details>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <p><strong>Scenario C.</strong> Your walk-forward backtest shows Sharpe = 1.8 across 8 years of OOS test windows. A naive single hold-out split (70/30) on the same data shows Sharpe = 2.4. Which should you trust for estimating live performance, and why is the other number misleading?</p>
  <details>
    <summary>Show answer →</summary>
    <div class="ans">
      <strong>Trust the walk-forward Sharpe = 1.8.</strong>
      <br><br>
      The walk-forward result aggregates 8 years of independent monthly test windows — the model was refitted before each. It experienced bull markets, bear markets, and sideways periods as they actually arrived, using only past data to predict each. This is the closest simulation of live trading.
      <br><br>
      The single hold-out Sharpe = 2.4 is misleading because: (a) the 30% test window might coincide with a favorable period (e.g., a bull market), inflating the number; (b) the 70% training period was chosen by the analyst — any reasonable-sounding choice of <code>train_frac</code> produces a different number; (c) there is only one test estimate, so uncertainty is high. The walk-forward aggregate is a far more stable and honest estimate.
    </div>
  </details>
</div>

